<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ayyubid Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ayyubid dynasty, Ayyubids, Ayyubid Sultanate, Ayyubid</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ayyubid Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim dynasty founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1171 and united Egypt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Saladin's victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a> in 1187 led to the recapture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>. After his death the realm was divided among family branches, and in 1250 it was overthrown in Egypt by its own mamluk soldiers, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire.jpg" alt="Ayyubid Empire" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayyubid_Sultanate_1193_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
